under the current Task-1 contract;

or, if the miss is demonstrably only timing alignment,
treat that as a measurement-design lesson
for future preregistered experiments
without rewriting LU-2E's failed result.

PLAIN-SPEAK INTERPRETATION

The 25-percent neural recruiter almost repeated its first result.

Nine new worlds passed everything.

The tenth world did not fail in the ordinary sense.

It finished exactly the same number
of new Phase-4 jobs.

It ended with less backlog.

It completed more jobs overall.

It used slightly fewer operations per completed job.

But a couple of completions landed
on different sides of the frozen final-window timing boundary.

That made the paired final-window score
12.5 points lower instead of the allowed 10.

The rule was frozen,
so confirmation fails.

We do not move the line afterward.

But before abandoning the mechanism,
we should determine whether this is
a real sustained capacity deficit
or simply a timing-window effect
created by two early recruitment differences.

